            REFSEQ INFORMATION: The reference sequence is identical to
            J01764.1.
            pT181 occurs naturally in Staphylococcus aureus and encodes
            inducible resistance to tetracycline. It contains four open reading
            frames encoding four putative polypeptides A, 37.5 kDa; B, 35 kDa;
            C, 23 kDa, and D, 18 kDa [3]. Polypeptide A is the repC protein [2]
            (bases 4289-794). Polypeptides B and D (bases 956-1843 and
            1800-2258, respectively) are involved in tetracycline resistance
            [3]. There is no known function for polypeptide C and deletion of a
            large portion of it causes no noticeable changes [3]. The
            recombination site RS-A consists of 24 bp of identity (the core,
            positions 2448-2472) flanked by some 50 base pairs of decreasing
            homology with other small Staphylococcal plasmids [4]. The
            recombination at RS-A is recA-independent and is mediated by a
            plasmid-encoded, transacting protein, Pre (plasmid recombination)
            [4].  Pre-mediated recombination is site specific in that it occurs
            within the core sequence of RS-A in a recA host [4].  Recombination
            also occurs between two intramolecular RS-A sites [4].  Pre-RS-A is
            not involved in plasmid maintenance [4].
            Replication, originating in a 168 bp span, proceeds
            unidirectionally and clockwise (anticlockwise as the map is
            conventionally drawn) [2].
            Polypeptide C has been changed to the longer Pre protein by
            revisions given in [1].
            The annotation was added by the NCBI Prokaryotic Genome Annotation
            Pipeline (PGAP). Information about PGAP can be found here:
            https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/genome/annotation_prok/
            COMPLETENESS: full length.
